<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wassoulou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wassoulou is a savanna region on the borders of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Côte d'Ivoire</a>, around the upper Sankarani River. Its people, largely of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a> origin but speaking the Maninka language, gave their name to the Wassoulou Empire founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Ture</a> in the late 19th century. The region is also known for its musical tradition, the source of Wassoulou music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wassoulou.jpg" alt="Wassoulou" width="282" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: T L Miles at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wassoulou_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wassoulou, also spelled Wassulu, Wassalou or Ouassalou, is a cultural area and historical region around the point where the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> meet, bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a> to the north-west and the Sankarani River to the east. Its people are called Wassulu, Wassulunka or Wassulunke. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> among them are believed to have come from the Fouta Djallon highlands and to have merged with the indigenous Mandé peoples before the 18th century, taking up local customs as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the 19th century the region is poorly attested, but it appears to have been a fairly decentralised and egalitarian society of jamana, alliances of small walled villages. In the 18th and early 19th centuries it was in some respects tributary to the Segou Empire, yet it still suffered regular slave raids. In 1870 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Ture</a> overthrew an older Wassoulou state whose faama, or ruler, was Dyanabufarina Modi, and imposed a hierarchical government, appointing the local Muslim convert Farbalay Jakite as his representative in 1882.</p><p>The Wassoulunke rebelled several times. A rising in 1885 against the institution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the suppression of animist practices was brutally put down by Samori's brother Keme Brema, and another after his failure at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikasso">Sikasso</a> was also crushed. His war with Kenedougou left thousands of refugees, many sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> or selling themselves to avoid starving. In 1891 he forced much of the population east with him and massacred the town of N'Tentou when its people refused to leave, so that his years left the region almost entirely depopulated.</p><p>Instability, including predation by colonial troops, continued under French rule. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> slowly died out in the French Sudan, tens of thousands of freed slaves returned to Wassoulou in the decades before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>About 160,000 people live in Wassoulou. It is the birthplace of Wassoulou music, a style that joins traditional and modern influences, with strong female singers and a pentatonic hunter's harp; Oumou Sangare, Ramata Diakite and Coumba Sidibe are among its singers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wassoulou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Samori Toure, a Maninka trader turned soldier, built a following in the upper Niger region from the 1860s, conquering small chiefdoms in the <b>Wassoulou</b> and Konyan areas.</blockquote><a href="/state/wassoulou-empire">Wassoulou Empire</a></li></ul></section>
